<DocLayout
  noindex={true}
  title="File details"
  description="How to read image file details in Image Identifier — camera fields, software tags, and generation clues."
  updated="2026-10-14"
>
  <p>
    Many image files carry extra fields: camera make and model, software names, timestamps, and
    sometimes generation-related text. Image Identifier groups readable fields so you can inspect
    them without treating any single tag as proof.
  </p>

  <h2>Useful categories</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Camera / capture</strong> — device or exposure-related fields when present.</li>
    <li><strong>Software &amp; editing</strong> — applications that may have written or edited the file.</li>
    <li><strong>Possible generation clues</strong> — fields that resemble generation tooling (labeled as clues).</li>
    <li><strong>Location</strong> — hidden by default if the file may include location data.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Interpretation rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Missing fields do not mean the image is AI-generated.</li>
    <li>Camera fields do not prove the image is unedited or authentic.</li>
    <li>A software tag may indicate editing rather than original capture.</li>
    <li>Generation-related text is a clue, not cryptographic proof.</li>
    <li>Fields can be copied, stripped, or forged.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Privacy tip</h2>
  <p>
    Location fields can be sensitive. Image Identifier keeps them hidden until you choose to reveal
    them. See also the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> page.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
